Answer 1: To identify the groups of McCabe and Halstead metrics most directly linked to software defects:

- Complexity Descriptors (McCabe metrics):
  - v(g) (cyclomatic complexity): Measures the number of independent paths through the code, associated with defect-proneness.
  - ev(g) (essential complexity): Captures structuredness — higher values may indicate harder-to-maintain code.
  - iv(g) (design complexity): Measures inter-module dependencies and intricacy in design, often related to quality.

- Size Descriptors (Halstead metrics):
  - loc (line count of code): Indicates the size of the module — larger modules are generally more defect-prone.
  - n (total operators + operands): Represents the total program size.
  - uniq_Op and uniq_Opnd (unique operators/operands): Capture program uniqueness/density.
  - lOCode, lOComment, lOBlank: Individual breakdowns of code representation.

- Effort Descriptors (Halstead metrics):
  - e (effort): Represents the total effort required to develop/understand the code — high effort may correlate with more defects.
  - d (difficulty), t (time estimator): Complementary to effort, reflecting maintenance or development challenges.
  - i (intelligence): Measures how difficult it is to implement the code — lower intelligence often contributes to higher defect risk.

These groups (complexity, size, effort) will guide feature engineering to create stronger defect predictors.

